/*
Problem: Validate Binary Search Tree (LC 98)
Topic: Binary Search Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

A valid BST must satisfy:

• Every node in the left subtree is
  smaller than the current node.

• Every node in the right subtree is
  greater than the current node.

Instead of comparing only with the
parent, keep track of the valid
minimum and maximum ancestors for
each node.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Start with no bounds.

2. For every node:

   • If it is <= minimum bound,
     return false.

   • If it is >= maximum bound,
     return false.

3. Recur for:

   • Left subtree with current node
     as the new maximum.

   • Right subtree with current node
     as the new minimum.

4. If every node satisfies its
   bounds, return true.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

        2
       / \
      1   3

Answer:

true

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

        5
       / \
      1   4
         / \
        3   6

Answer:

false

Reason:

3 is in the right subtree of 5 but
3 < 5, violating the BST property.

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Each recursive call carries the
allowed value range for that node.

A node must lie strictly between its
minimum and maximum bounds to satisfy
the BST property.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Every node is visited once.

Space : O(h)

h = Height of the tree due to the
recursion stack.

Worst case: O(n)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Binary Search Tree / Recursion
*/

class Solution {
public:

    bool isBST(TreeNode* root,
               TreeNode* min,
               TreeNode* max) {

        if (root == NULL)
            return true;

        // Violates lower bound
        if (min != NULL &&
            root->val <= min->val)
            return false;

        // Violates upper bound
        if (max != NULL &&
            root->val >= max->val)
            return false;

        // Check left and right subtrees
        return isBST(root->left,
                     min,
                     root) &&
               isBST(root->right,
                     root,
                     max);
    }

    bool isValidBST(TreeNode* root) {

        return isBST(root, NULL, NULL);
    }
};
